<DocsLayout title="Cross-project recall in Haft v9" description="The bounded local decision-summary index, its evidence boundary, and its separation from v9 typed project memory.">
  <h1>Cross-project recall</h1>

  <p>
    Haft retains a user-local cross-project index at
    <code>~/.haft/index.db</code>. It contains decision summaries from local
    Haft projects. When a new ProblemCard is framed, matching decisions from
    other projects may appear as <strong>Cross-Project History</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>What is indexed</h2>

  <p>
    After a DecisionRecord is created, Haft can index its project identity,
    decision identity, title, selected option, rationale summary, weakest link,
    primary language, and creation time. The index does not copy the entire
    project graph.
  </p>

  <p>
    The baseline path uses local FTS5 over summaries. Results exclude the
    current project and carry a context label: CL2 for the same detected primary
    language or CL1 for a different one. That label is an evidence congruence
    cue, not proof that the old decision applies here.
  </p>

  <h2>How to use a recalled decision</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>Treat the result as related history, not a recommendation.</li>
    <li>Open the source project's exact decision before relying on its rationale.</li>
    <li>Compare the old and current constraints, evidence, scope, and weakest link.</li>
    <li>Frame or decide the current project object through its own authority path.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Retrieval rank never selects an option, merges an identity, or transfers
    authority from one project to another.
  </p>

  <h2>Not typed project memory</h2>

  <p>
    Cross-project recall and v9 typed memory are separate systems. Typed memory
    resolves an exact <code>EntityOfConcern</code> inside one pinned project
    snapshot and reads a bounded neighborhood around it. A cross-project
    summary cannot substitute for that exact identity or be admitted as a
    project relation by rank.
  </p>

  <h2>Optional embedding compatibility</h2>

  <p>
    The retained <code>haft-embed</code> sidecar may augment older semantic
    recall paths and degrades to FTS when unavailable. This compatibility path
    is outside the v9 contract and P14 acceptance basis. Haft makes no v9 claim
    that dense or hybrid retrieval is better than FTS, <code>rg</code>, a pinned
    source file, or host RAG.
  </p>

  <h2>Local boundary</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>The index stays on the user's machine.</li>
    <li>It is not shared automatically through git.</li>
    <li>Different engineers can have different indexed projects and freshness.</li>
    <li>Missing results do not prove that no related decision exists.</li>
  </ul>

